let's get some reaction to that  interview and the review uh we're joined  now by caroline noakes the chair of the  women and equalities committee at  westminster thank you for being with us  um we just heard there some of the  details of lady dorian's review into  making the  whole justice system easier for victims  of rape and serious sexual offences  would you like to see some of these  changes apply  throughout the uk well i think it's  important to reflect that scotland has a  separate  justice system but it's absolutely  crucial that we find  effective ways to support victims  through the criminal justice  system whether they be male or female  and  there have been good initiatives put in  place the independent sexual violence  advisors  who are supporting complainants through  the whole process and being with them  in court but i think there are some  really significant problems  about encouraging victims to come  forward making sure that police make  referrals to the cps  making sure that the cps has the  confidence to take things to trial that  we see  not just more convictions but more  prosecutions you won't get the  convictions  without the prosecutions in the first  place and i really  think it's time that the government look  closely at having a women's justice  strategy  so that we can see victims supported  from the  start to the end of the process yeah as  you say there are some clear  issues with the process not enough women  coming forward not enough referring to  the cps  and then if you look at the last three  years rape conviction is more than  halving  in england and wales i mean one of the  most controversial suggestions from lady  dorian is the fact that actually some of  these trials  should be heard not by juries who may be  vulnerable to  rape myths for example but by trained  judges i mean is that the kind of road  that we need to go down  if we're going to get serious about  getting convictions  well we have a long history and  principle  of crimes being tried by a jury of your  peers in this country  and i think that that is important but i  also think it's right that we have a  full and frank conversation about how we  can do things better how perhaps we can  do things quicker i thought  one of the most important points that  lady dorian made was about victims  having to relive their trauma  potentially  many times and over a long period and i  think  that we need to find a way that the  whole process can be speeded up  so that victims see cases coming to  court  much more quickly and then have the  opportunity to move on with the rest of  their lives  it does feel doesn't it that one of the  positive  things after a really grueling couple of  weeks for so many women has been  that we are starting to have a more full  and frank conversation about the kind of  harassment and the kind of assault as  well that some people face  and so many women do see as a fabric of  their lives you  recently gave an interview where you  said a labour mp tried to grape you  i mean talk about your own experience if  you want to obviously don't feel that  you have to  but how much of an issue do you think  this is for  for all women the kind of things that  we've been hearing  it's too common in every walk of life in  every um and this will be shocking  people here in every school playground  in every university in every workplace  this is going on far too often and when  you read the statistics about the number  of young women  that have been subject to sexual assault  or harassment  they're horrific and that's why i'm very  happy to co-sign amendments with people  like harriet harman  to make sexual harassment in a public  place uh outlawed  i think that that's really important we  have to change the culture  and that's why my committee has uh in  this last week launched  an inquiry into the cultures that are  underpinning  male violence against women i think it's  crucial that we  don't just um don't just pontificate on  this we need some practical solutions  and we need them quickly whether that be  through  the court system whether that's through  education whether we have a  candid conversation about pornography  the ease of access to it for young  people  and what that's doing to give them a  completely skewed  impression of what adult sexual  relationships are like  talking about changing the culture the  sunday mirror has a story this morning  about the culture within the police  and former police officers criticizing  sexism and  misogyny sir peter fahey who's the  former chief constable of quote in  manchester police said that if a member  of his family was attacked  he would probably advise him not to  report it he also said  there's no way his daughter would join  the police force  is there a problem of sexism in the  police  well i think that's a matter for the  police themselves to  investigate and look at um but reports  like that advising your children not to  join the police  not wanting to report crimes i think we  have a really important  job to do here empowering women who are  victims of crime  to go to the police to know that they  will be believed that they will have  their cases listened to sympathetically  i know here in hampshire police there  are more than 100 officers  working on this sort of crime and it's  treated with great sensitivity and  care but that's led by a female chief  constable who is absolutely committed to  doing that  i think it's absolutely vital that we  have to find  better ways to give women confidence and  when you look at the the statistics  around  sexual violence the statistics around  sexual harassment  unfortunately we're letting far too many  women down  okay thank you so much for coming on the  program this morning and caroline next  there thank you
 

